Janis responds:
> It should be at least (0:1), shouldn't it?

He may have used one wish already, engrave-identifying the
wand, to verify that it wasn't a wand of death.  (If he had
formally identified it, he would know how many charges it has.)

> I've done the castle (but not the rooms in the back of the
> castle yet because I don't have levitation) and the wishing
> wand from there is 0:1.  I happen to have a scroll of
> charging, means of blessing it, and a magic marker to write
> others (it's charged at 0:78 or something like that).

I assume if you can bless the scroll of charging you can also
bless blank scrolls.  I'm also guessing you have adequate
fountains for dipping as many otherwise-worthless scrolls
as necessary.

> So all in all I expect to get 5 wishes out of the castle
> wand, and at least 4 out of the one from the shop, and that's
> a lot of wishes.

It is.  I would try to save at least one of the wands at (0:2)
or better for late-game contingencies, if possible.

> I'd appriciate some advice on what to wish for.

In general, once you have the key basic equipment you need,
magic markers are often very good wishes, especially for
non-spellcasting roles.  They're so...  flexible.

> My priorities are:
>  - ring of levitation

You will *probably* find that eventually, assuming you have
adequate sources of identification to do every ring you find.

>  - ring of free action

This is more arguable, as a matter of style.  Some players
would make this a high priority.

Personally, I find that I can seldom spare a ring slot for it,
especially in the late game.  I tend to wear slow digestion and
regeneration, for the stamina it gives me, the ability to
persist in a protracted combat scenario without keeling over
from hunger or HP loss.  But again, that's very much a matter
of style.  Some players make a point of *not* being in combat
for extended periods of time.

Ring slot strategy is, in general, a matter of personal choice.

> I already have a ring of teleport control and a ring of
> conflict.

Eat tengu.  The sooner you can get intrinsic control and free
up that ring slot for something else, the better.

Conflict can admittedly be very useful, if you aren't trying to
retain multiple pets.  (The technical strategic value of pets,
of course, declines sharply during the middle game and begins
in the late game to asymptotically approach zero.  However,
keeping pets has a certain emotional appeal, especially if
they've done a lot to help you *reach* the late game.)

> Armorwise, I have a GDSM, speed boots and elven cloak, as
> well as basic gloves, a basic helm and a blue and green
> shield.  Amuletwise, I have "oR and "oLS (I'm currently
> wearing "oR).

As a lawful character, you could wish for lightweight slotless
MR (e.g., the Magic Mirror), but the GDSM would be redundant
then, so you'd want SDSM; making that swap would consume at
least two wishes, plus additional resources if the GDSM is
already enchanted above +2, so it may not be worth it.

> So I was thinking - shield of reflection (to be able to put
> "oLS on) - cloak of protection - a +3 T-shirt

OTOH, if you value life saving enough to wish for a shield of
reflection, the value of the slotless MR and SDSM goes up by at
least one wish.  How many artifacts have you seen so far?

> That's five wishes and I may have 4 left. Should I keep them
> in case I need them in the endgame?

Try to keep at least one, preferably two, non-wrest wishes
back for emergencies.

> For instance to wish for a cockatrice corpse in astral? Or to
> wish for a helm of opposite alignment? (I'm lawful)

As a lawful melee-oriented character, if you can handle the
Wizard and the Sanctum and the treck back up out of Gehennom,
you won't need a helm of opposite alignment.  The astral plane
will be, in a word, anticlimactic.  Just whack death or famine
or whoever twice with your +6 artifact weapon and watch him
keel over.  It's much easier than killing the Wizard after you
run out of wand-of-death charges.

> Maybe wish for scrolls of genocide

Only do this if you collected and identified all the available
scrolls and have still not seen genocide yet.  Otherwise, the
magic marker is a *much* more efficient way to go.

> and maybe another magic marker

A magic marker is seldom a bad use for a wish.  You can do so
many different useful things with them (especially if you have
all three beautitudes of water available).  Genocide.  Reverse
genocide.  Enchanting.  Charging.  Fooproofing (and repair).
Curse removal.  Even taming.

> to massively extinct annoying species (;, L, R, ...).

I usually do mind flayers, master mind flayers, L, and R.

I can definitely understand why someone might want to do ; as
well.

> I'm a dwarf so I can't genocide both types of mind flayers
> with a blessed scroll of genocide, right?

Technically you *can*, but it is not the traditionally
recommended technique, since it would kill you too.

For some roles, with a second wand of wishing, I would
recommend a blessed spellbook of identify if you haven't
already found one (unless you have a pretty big stack of
identify scrolls), but I'm not sure a Caveman will be able to
cast the level-three spell with a sane failure rate (the only
worse role for spellcasting is Barbarian), so that may not be
a worthwhile wish use in your case.


